An embedded language usually starts as a convenience: let users write the part of an application that changes quickly without rebuilding the host. The interpreter is only part of the design. A host that runs scripts must also determine what a script can observe, what it can modify, and how long host objects stay usable. Those choices apply whether the guest is dynamically typed, compiled to bytecode ahead of time, or interpreted as syntax.

\section{Distinguish a Scripting Use from a Language Category}

Chanchal K. Roy and James R. Cordy's \emph{Are Scripting Languages Really Different?} compares code cloning in Python projects with earlier measurements for C, C\#, and Java. Its evidence is about duplication in the projects studied, not a universal definition of scripting. What matters to an embedding design is the direction of control. In Olin Shivers's \emph{A Scheme Shell}, Scheme drives composition of external processes through a process notation with I/O redirections. In an application extension, the host usually chooses when to call the guest and which operations the guest receives. A single language can serve both roles; the default authority in the two settings should not be inferred from the syntax.

\section{Define a Host Interface, Not a Bag of Primitives}

Yu Zhang's \emph{Language Embedding and Foreign Function Interface} distinguishes embedding and foreign calls and illustrates their implementation with several guest languages. At a foreign boundary, specify conversion of values, ownership of arguments and results, error mapping, and thread requirements. At an application boundary, add the list of available effects. A plug-in that chooses a caption can be given a record of document metadata and a callback that returns text; it does not automatically need a raw reference to the document store or a filesystem accessor.

A budget for guest evaluation is distinct from a budget on host work. Counting interpreter steps bounds only execution measured by that counter. If one host call traverses an entire document, either account for that work, expose a bounded operation, or give the host operation its own deadline with defined cancellation behavior. Similarly, limiting the guest's heap will not help if a tiny guest object can cause the host to retain many large objects. An embedder should charge both the stored guest values and the host resources that the guest keeps live. The \href{dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/34-capability-secured-compiler-ir.md}{dossier's Capability-Secured Compiler IR chapter} explores making authority explicit in compiler representations; explicit authority must also survive marshalling at the host interface.

\section{Worked Example: A Caption Callback}

Suppose a layout engine asks a script for a caption twice, once per document revision. The first invocation gets a read-only metadata handle and returns a guest string. Stable ownership rules might say that the handle is borrowed for one call, and that the string is copied into host storage before the call ends. If the guest stores the metadata handle and calls it during the second revision, a validity check must return a stale-handle error, not silently read the first revision or resolve the slot to a different document. One practical representation is an opaque token keyed to a slot and a generation, together with the actual permission checked by the host. Return an explicit failure value for a guest exception; do not expose a half-applied document change.

The implementation has a second lifetime problem. As Marcus Crestani explains in \emph{Foreign-Function Interfaces for Garbage-Collected Programming Languages}, values referenced from native code must remain visible to the collector, and a moving collector may have to update those references. A raw pointer to the guest string is not necessarily a stable handle. Keep a registered reference while conversion can trigger garbage collection, and copy through the runtime interface before releasing the reference. For an asynchronous callback, retain its registered handle until the last in-flight call finishes, not merely until unregister is requested.

Test by forcing a collection between return and copy, attempting to use the expired metadata handle after slot reuse, and independently exhausting guest steps and host-side work. Both success and failure must release the temporary references. The observable invariant is that each callback returns an owned caption or a defined error, and cannot see metadata outside the revision and permissions supplied for that invocation.

