\section{Commit Guest-Supplied Edits Only After Whole-Batch Validation}

An embedding need not choose between read-only scripts and giving the guest direct access to document setters. For an illustrative annotation plug-in, let the script produce a bounded list of proposed edits against a snapshot. The host copies the proposals into ordinary host data, validates each against the granted operations and snapshot, and applies them to a private draft. Assume validation and draft edits invoke no guest callbacks and have no external effects. At publication, compare the current revision with the snapshot revision and replace the document in one atomic host operation. The outcome is a new revision containing all requested changes, or an explicit error with no document changes attributable to this invocation.

\begin{verbatim}
run_plugin(plugin, snapshot, grant):
  proposals = guest_call_and_copy(plugin, snapshot, grant)
  if proposals is Error(e): return e
  if size(proposals) > grant.max_edits: return TooManyEdits
  draft = private_copy(snapshot)
  for edit in proposals:
    # Inspect resolved targets, not guest spellings alone.
    if not allowed(edit, draft, grant): return Denied
    if not apply_checked(draft, edit): return BadEdit
  # An intervening host change wins over this draft.
  return publish_if_current(snapshot.revision, draft)
\end{verbatim}

At revision \(7\), give a plug-in permission to edit captions only. It proposes changing the caption from \texttt{north} to \texttt{east} and changing the document title to \texttt{Maps}. Validation returns \texttt{Denied}; the published caption is still \texttt{north} at revision \(7\). With only the caption edit, the result is revision \(8\) with caption \texttt{east}. If another editor first publishes revision \(8\) with caption \texttt{west}, the plug-in instead gets \texttt{Conflict}, and \texttt{west} stays published.

The publication boundary in Figure~\ref{fig:visual-185} distinguishes changing the draft from changing the document seen by readers.

